<exercises xml:id="ex-continuous-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Exercises related to continuous functions and homeomorphisms</title>
<p>
    We list a large number of small exercises for practice. Some of the exercises are taken from <sq>Elementary topology problem textbook</sq> by Viro et.al.
</p>
<exercisegroup>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            State whether the following statements are true or false. Justify your answers.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                If <m>f\colon A\to B</m> is a function then, <m>f^{-1}(X\cup Y)=f^{-1}(X)\cup f^{-1}(Y)</m> for any subsets <m>X,Y</m> of <m>B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>a\in f^{-1}(X\cup Y)</m>, i.e., <m>f(a)\in X\cup Y</m>, i.e., <m>a\in f^{-1}(X)\cup f^{-1}(Y)</m>. Conversely, if <m>a\in f^{-1}(Y)</m> then, <m>f(a)\in Y</m> and hence, <m>f(a)\in X\cup Y</m>, i.e., <m>a\in f^{-1}(X\cup Y)</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                There does not exist a non-constant continuous function <m>f\colon\R_\ell\to\R</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Take the identity function and use the fact that the lower limit topology is finer than the usual topology.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                Let <m>f\colon\Z_{\geq 0}\to\R</m> be given by <m>f(n)=1/n</m> for <m>n\neq 0</m> and <m>f(0)=0</m>. Then, <m>f</m> is continuous when <m>\Z_{\geq 0}</m> is given the subspace topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                The induced topology on <m>\Z_{\geq 0}</m> is discrete and hence <em>any</em> map from <m>\Z_{\geq 0}</m> is continuous.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Let <m>\mathbb{L}=\{0\}\cup\{1/n:n\in\N\}</m>. If <m>\mathbb{L}</m> and <m>\Z_{\geq 0}</m> are given the subspace topology induced from <m>\R</m> then, the function <m>f\colon\mathbb{L}\to\Z_{\geq 0}</m> given by <m>f(0)=0</m> and <m>f(1/n)=n</m> whenever <m>n\neq 0</m> is continuous.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                In the subspace topology <m>\Z_{\geq 0}</m> is a discrete topological space, in particular, <m>\{0\}</m> is an open subset. The preimage of <m>\{0\}</m>, <m>f^{-1}(0)=\{0\}</m> which is not open in <m>\mathbb{L}</m>.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                The map <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> defined by <m>T(x,y)=(-y,x)</m> is continuous. 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Note that the given linear map is anticlockwise rotation by <m>90^0</m>. In particular, it preserve distances. 
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                Let <m>a,b,c,d\in\R</m>. If <m>f\colon [a,b]\to[c,d]</m> is a strictly monotone continuous surjective function then, <m>f</m> is a homeomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                The function <m>f\colon\R\to (0,1)</m> given by <m>x\mapsto\frac{e^x}{1+e^x}</m> is a homeomorphism
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                If every real-valued function defined on <m>X</m> is continuous then, <m>X</m> has a discrete topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                By the hypothesis, for each <m>x\in X</m>, the function <m>\delta_x(y)=0</m> if <m>y\neq x</m> and <m>\delta_x(x)=1</m> must be continuous. Thus, <m>\{x\}</m> is both open (it is the complement of a closed set <m>\delta_x^{-1}\{0\}</m>) and closed (it is the preimage of a closed set <m>\{1\}</m>).
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                The folding map <m>\R^2\to\R^2</m> given by <m>(x,y)\mapsto(x,|y|)</m> is open.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Consider the image of the unit square centered around the origin. 
            </p>
            <!--<p>
                The folding map is a closed map. Indeed, let <m>A</m> be a closed set and let <m>(a,b)</m> be a limit point of <m>f(A)</m>. Then, there is a sequence <m>(x_n,y_n)\in A</m> such that <m>(x_n,|y_n|)\to (a,b)</m>.
            </p>-->
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                The map <m>z\mapsto z^3</m> of the plane to itself is not closed.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Use sequences with the fact that every bounded sequence in the plane has a convergent subsequence.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                The inverse map <m>\mathbb{S}^1\to\mathbb{S}^1</m> given by <m>e^{i\theta}\to e^{-i\theta}</m> is continuous.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                Following two figures are homeomorphic.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="circle-with-spike">
                <caption>Circle with spike and circle</caption>
                <image width="50%">
                    <shortdescription></shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75pt,y=0.75pt,yscale=-1,xscale=1]
%uncomment if require: \path (0,300); %set diagram left start at 0, and has height of 300

%Shape: Circle [id:dp4789073543962954] 
\draw   (222,151) .. controls (222,137.19) and (233.19,126) .. (247,126) .. controls (260.81,126) and (272,137.19) .. (272,151) .. controls (272,164.81) and (260.81,176) .. (247,176) .. controls (233.19,176) and (222,164.81) .. (222,151) -- cycle ;
%Shape: Circle [id:dp6978811644782112] 
\draw   (100,152) .. controls (100,138.19) and (111.19,127) .. (125,127) .. controls (138.81,127) and (150,138.19) .. (150,152) .. controls (150,165.81) and (138.81,177) .. (125,177) .. controls (111.19,177) and (100,165.81) .. (100,152) -- cycle ;
%Straight Lines [id:da13801441628430933] 
\draw    (272,151) -- (311,151) ;




\end{tikzpicture}

                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                An open disk in <m>\R^2</m> is homeomorphic to <m>\R^2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                One may consider an open unit disc and then the following mapping.
                <me>
                    (x,y)\mapsto \frac{1}{\lVert(x,y)\rVert+1}\cdot (x,y)
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="no">
            <p>
                The spaces <m>\Z</m> and <m>\Q</m> are homeomorphic when considered as subspaces of <m>\R</m> with the usual topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                One is a discrete space and other is not.
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement correct="yes">
            <p>
                The spaces <m>\R</m> and <m>\mathbb{S}^1</m> are not homeomorphic.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
            <p>
                Note that <m>\mathbb{S}^1</m> is compact but <m>\R</m> is not a compact space. Alternatively, removing one point from <m>\R</m> one gets a disconnected space but removing one point from the circle one gets a connected space. 
            </p>
        </feedback>
    </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
</exercises>